  });
  var hormuzX = xp(5);
  svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:hormuzX,x2:hormuzX,y1:margin.top,y2:margin.top+PH,stroke:"#1a3560","stroke-opacity":"0.5","stroke-dasharray":"2,3"}));
  svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:hormuzX+3,y:margin.top+20,"font-size":"7","font-weight":"700","fill":"#1a3560"}, "HORMUZ"));
  svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:hormuzX+3,y:margin.top+29,"font-size":"7","font-weight":"700","fill":"#1a3560"}, "WAR BEGINS"));
  var bondPath = "";
  var mortPath = "";
  data.forEach(function(pt,i){
    var x = xp(i);
    var yB = yp(pt.bond);
    var yM = yp(pt.mort);
    bondPath += (i===0 ? "M" : "L") + x + "," + yB + " ";
    mortPath += (i===0 ? "M" : "L") + x + "," + yM + " ";
  });
  svg.appendChild(el("path",{d:mortPath, fill:"none", stroke:"#4a5568", "stroke-width":"2"}));
  svg.appendChild(el("path",{d:bondPath, fill:"none", stroke:"#2e7d32", "stroke-width":"2"}));
  var lastX = xp(n-1);
  var lastYBond = yp(data[n-1].bond);
  var lastYMort = yp(data[n-1].mort);
  svg.appendChild(el("circle",{cx:lastX,cy:lastYBond,r:4,fill:"#4a5568"}));
  svg.appendChild(el("circle",{cx:lastX,cy:lastYMort,r:4,fill:"#4a5568"}));
  svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:xp(n-2)-4,y:lastYMort-8,"text-anchor":"end","font-size":"7","font-weight":"700","fill":"#4a5568"}, "5Y FIXED"));
  svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:xp(n-2)-4,y:lastYBond+12,"text-anchor":"end","font-size":"7","font-weight":"700","fill":"#2e7d32"}, "GOC 5Y"));
  var pillW = 50, pillH = 16;
  var pillX = lastX - pillW - 6;
  var pillY = lastYMort - pillH/2 - 14;
  if (pillX < margin.left) pillX = lastX + 6;
  svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:pillX,y:pillY,width:pillW,height:pillH,rx:3,fill:"#e8a825"}));
  svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:pillX+pillW/2,y:pillY+pillH/2+4,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"9","font-weight":"700","fill":"#111111"}, "4.93%"));
  var container = _cs ? _cs.parentNode : null;
  if (container) container.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Bank of Canada selected bond yields, Trading Economics, nesto.ca rate tracking, June 29, 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The spread between the GoC 5-year yield and the average discounted 5-year fixed mortgage rate has held near 190 basis points since the Hormuz conflict began, wider than the typical 100 to 150 basis point spread seen before February. Source: Bank of Canada, nesto.ca.</p>
<h2>What to Do Before the Renewal Notice Arrives</h2>
<p>Lenders typically send renewal notices 90 to 120 days before maturity, and most allow a rate hold to be locked in well ahead of that date. For clients with a 2021 vintage 5-year fixed approaching renewal in the back half of 2026, the planning window is now, not when the notice lands. Comparing the lender''s renewal offer against the broader market, confirming whether a blend-and-extend option makes sense given the IRD penalty math, and timing any portfolio rebalancing to coincide with the payment change are all conversations that belong in this quarter, not after the new rate is already locked.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients renewing from 2021-era 5-year fixed mortgages are bracing for a number they have been dreading for months, and many assumed the recent BoC holds meant rates had stopped moving against them. Discovering that fixed rates are still elevated because of bond yields, not the policy rate, often lands as a fresh frustration rather than expected news.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to walk through your renewal now, before the lender''s notice arrives, because the number you''re going to see is going to feel disconnected from what you''ve been reading about the Bank of Canada holding rates. That hold matters for variable mortgages, but your fixed rate is priced off the government bond market, and bond yields have stayed elevated because of the situation in the Middle East, specifically how it''s affecting inflation expectations. The Big Six average on a 5-year fixed right now is just under 5%, which is a real increase from the 1.5% range many 2021 originations carried. Let''s look at your specific numbers, what the payment change looks like, and whether there''s anything in your non-registered accounts we should be doing in tandem with this renewal to soften the impact.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with 5-year fixed mortgages originated in 2020 or 2021 at sub-2% rates, renewing in the second half of 2026 into rates near 5%.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with variable-rate, variable-payment mortgages, who have already absorbed most of their rate increase and are now watching the July 15 BoC decision more than bond yields.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with significant non-registered unrealized losses who can pair tax-loss harvesting with their renewal timeline to generate cash flow relief.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull the renewal dates for all clients with mortgages maturing in Q3 or Q4 2026 and flag the 2021-vintage 5-year fixed cohort specifically.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For each flagged client, confirm whether their lender has issued a rate hold offer and compare it against the current Big Six average and broker market rates.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review non-registered accounts for tax-loss harvesting opportunities that could be timed alongside the renewal, respecting the 30-day superficial loss window.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients actively switching lenders this week, confirm whether the RBC or CIBC June 30 promotional deadlines apply to their situation.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your mortgage renewal and why the BoC hold has not helped your rate<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our conversation about your upcoming renewal. As discussed, the Bank of Canada''s rate holds affect variable mortgages directly, but your fixed rate is priced off Government of Canada bond yields, which have stayed elevated due to inflation expectations tied to the conflict in the Middle East.<br><br>
The current Big Six average on a 5-year fixed conventional mortgage is just under 5%, against the lowest insured broker rates near 4%. I would recommend confirming your lender''s rate hold offer and comparing it against the broader market before you lock in.<br><br>
Let''s also revisit whether any tax-loss harvesting in your non-registered account makes sense to help offset the payment change.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Self-directed mortgage holders renewing in late 2026:</strong> have not had a financial planning conversation tied to their renewal and are likely shopping rate alone, without considering tax-loss harvesting or broader portfolio coordination.</p>
<p><strong>Recent first-time buyers comparing fixed and variable options:</strong> are weighing today''s 4.04% to 4.93% fixed range against 3.30% to 3.45% variable rates without a framework for the trade-off between certainty and flexibility.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I noticed mortgage renewal notices have been going out for the back half of this year, and with bond yields still elevated because of the Hormuz situation, a lot of homeowners are seeing bigger jumps than they expected even with the Bank of Canada holding rates. Are you coming up for renewal, and have you had a chance to look at how it fits with the rest of your financial picture?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A homeowner shopping their renewal alone typically compares rate quotes in isolation, without connecting the decision to tax-loss harvesting opportunities, RRSP contribution timing, or the broader question of whether their overall portfolio allocation should shift alongside a meaningfully higher mortgage payment.</p>
<p>An advisor who understands both the bond market mechanics behind today''s fixed rates and the client''s full financial picture can turn a single rate-shopping decision into a coordinated plan, which is a service the prospect cannot get from a mortgage broker or a bank''s renewal department alone.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>When does your current mortgage term come up for renewal, and have you received a rate hold offer yet?</p>
<p>Are you planning to stay with your current lender, or are you open to shopping the renewal?</p>
<p>Has anyone walked you through how the higher payment might affect your other savings goals this year?</p>
<p>Do you have any non-registered investments where realizing a loss might make sense to offset other taxable income this year?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> If your mortgage renews this year, the BoC hold will not be the reason your rate moves<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
If you have a 5-year fixed mortgage renewing in the second half of 2026, you may be noticing that the Bank of Canada''s recent rate holds have not translated into lower renewal quotes. That is because fixed rates track bond yields, not the policy rate, and bond yields have stayed elevated due to the situation in the Middle East.<br><br>
The current Big Six average on a 5-year fixed is just under 5%, a significant jump for anyone renewing from a 2021-era rate near 1.5%.<br><br>
If it would help to walk through your renewal options alongside the rest of your financial plan, including any tax planning that might soften the payment change, I would be glad to set up a short call.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
