VALUES (
  '2026/06/30/hormuz-toll-free-window-expires-august-energy-portfolio-risk',
  'geo', 'article',
  'The Hormuz Truce Markets Are Trading Today Has an Expiry Date Few Are Pricing',
  'The toll-free window in the US-Iran memorandum runs out around August 16, and Tehran has already signaled what comes next.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The US-Iran memorandum signed June 17 guarantees toll-free passage through the Strait of Hormuz for 60 days only,</strong><span> a window that expires around August 16.</span></li>
<li><strong>Iranian officials have stated directly that they intend to introduce a transit fee once that window closes,</strong><span> a position the US disputes but has not resolved in writing.</span></li>
<li><strong>Renewed clashes over the weekend damaged two vessels</strong><span> before the latest truce and a fresh round of talks scheduled for this week in Doha, underlining how fragile the current calm remains.</span></li>
<li><strong>Saudi Arabia and the UAE have pipeline capacity to route around Hormuz entirely,</strong><span> while Kuwait, Qatar, and Bahrain do not, creating an uneven exposure across Gulf oil producers that has direct implications for Canadian energy positioning.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The Strait of Hormuz reopened on paper June 17, when President Trump and Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian signed a memorandum of understanding ending the active phase of the war. Markets have treated the reopening as resolved. The text of the agreement itself says otherwise: Iran''s commitment to toll-free passage runs for sixty days only, a window that expires around August 16.</p>
<p>What happens after that date is not a settled question. It is the next chapter of a dispute that has already shaped four months of oil prices, and the mechanism connecting it to Canadian portfolios is direct enough to trace in a single step.</p>
<h2>How This Connects to Canadian Portfolios</h2>
<p>Iranian officials have stated plainly that they intend to introduce a transit fee once the 60-day window closes. The memorandum itself does not foreclose this. It commits Iran only to toll-free passage during the window, after which Iran is to conduct dialogue with Oman, in discussion with other Gulf states, to define the strait''s future administration and maritime services. Secretary of State Marco Rubio has said Washington will not accept any toll. Iran''s Persian Gulf Strait Authority has said fees are merely suspended, not cancelled.</p>
<p>If Iran does impose a charge after August 16, even framed as a service fee rather than a toll, the practical effect on shipping costs is the same one the market priced during the worst of the conflict: war-risk insurance premiums that ran as high as 8% of vessel value during the closure, against a pre-war norm near 0.25%. WTI and Brent both carry a structural premium tied to this exact uncertainty, and Canadian energy producers, whose free cash flow has been a primary driver of the TSX''s 27% year-to-date sector gain, are directly exposed to whatever that premium does next.</p>
<h2>Tail Risk Versus Base Case</h2>
<p>The base case, reflected in this week''s truce and the scheduled Doha talks, is that diplomacy holds and a negotiated administration framework emerges that preserves toll-free or near-toll-free passage. The US has stated explicitly that Gulf states will not accept an arrangement that does not permit toll-free access, which gives Washington leverage Tehran cannot easily dismiss.</p>
<p>The tail risk is narrower but real: Iran asserts a unilateral fee, framed as a maritime service charge rather than a toll to avoid the clearer legal violation, and the Gulf states most exposed, Kuwait, Qatar, and Bahrain, are forced into a position where compliance is cheaper than confrontation. Council on Foreign Relations analyst Steven Cook has noted that Saudi Arabia and the UAE have built pipeline capacity, the East-West Pipeline and the Fujairah export terminal respectively, that lets them route oil around Hormuz entirely. Kuwait, Qatar, and Bahrain have no comparable alternative. That asymmetry means a partial fee regime is more plausible than an all-or-nothing outcome, since Iran would have leverage over a subset of producers even if the larger Gulf states can route around the dispute.</p>
