<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">WTI has not returned to the pre-war level near $75 at any point since February 28, even during the most optimistic ceasefire announcements. The 60-day toll-free window in the memorandum began June 17 and expires around August 16. Source: Trading Economics.</p>
<h2>What the Weekend''s Clashes Confirm</h2>
<p>Renewed fighting over the weekend, which damaged two vessels before the current truce, is a reminder that the underlying dispute over who controls the strait has not been resolved by the memorandum, only paused. Iranian state media has separately portrayed Tehran''s negotiated language on the strait''s future administration as a diplomatic win, while a senior US official has acknowledged that Iran will likely assert its rights as aggressively as it can in the Oman talks. Both governments are negotiating from positions that assume the other side will eventually concede ground neither has actually conceded yet.</p>
<h2>The Realistic Portfolio Implication</h2>
<p>This is not a call to reposition out of Canadian energy. The sector''s strength this year reflects genuine free cash flow generation at current oil prices, not merely a geopolitical risk premium that could evaporate. The realistic implication is narrower: the August 16 date belongs on the calendar alongside the BoC''s July 15 decision and the CUSMA review timeline, because a credible Iranian move to reimpose fees, even partial ones aimed at the smaller Gulf states, would reintroduce the kind of volatility WTI saw in March and April, with knock-on effects for Canadian energy equities, the CAD, and headline inflation readings the Bank of Canada is already watching closely.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients following the news likely believe the Hormuz situation is resolved, given the truce and the formal reopening announcement, and may be surprised or skeptical to learn the underlying dispute has a specific expiry date still ahead.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to flag something on the calendar that most coverage of the Hormuz situation has not emphasized. The agreement that reopened the strait guarantees toll-free shipping for sixty days only, which puts the real test of this deal around August 16, not today. Iran has said directly it intends to revisit fees once that window closes, and the US has said it will not accept that. Nothing about your energy positioning needs to change today, the fundamentals there are genuinely strong, but I am tracking that date alongside the other key dates on our radar, because a credible move by Iran to reimpose charges would likely bring back some of the volatility we saw in oil prices back in March and April.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated positions in Canadian energy producers whose share prices have tracked elevated oil prices through the conflict.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with diversified portfolios holding moderate energy sector weight as part of broader Canadian equity exposure.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients positioned in Canadian energy infrastructure and pipeline names that could benefit from continued elevated risk premiums even if direct Hormuz exposure stays contained.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Add August 16 to the internal calendar of key dates alongside the BoC July 15 decision and the CUSMA review timeline.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client portfolios for concentrated energy sector exposure and confirm position sizing reflects appropriate tail-risk tolerance.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Monitor the scheduled Doha talks this week for any signal on how the Iran-Oman administration framework is progressing.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a brief client-facing note distinguishing the base case, a negotiated framework preserving toll-free access, from the tail risk of a partial fee regime targeting smaller Gulf states.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Hormuz deal has a sixty-day clock, and it matters for your energy positioning<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our conversation about the Strait of Hormuz. The memorandum that reopened the strait guarantees toll-free passage for sixty days only, a window that began June 17 and runs to roughly August 16. Iranian officials have indicated they intend to revisit fees once that period ends, while the US has said it will not accept any toll.<br><br>
This is not a reason to change your current energy positioning, which reflects genuinely strong free cash flow fundamentals. It is a date worth having on our radar, since a credible move by Iran after August 16 could reintroduce volatility similar to what we saw earlier this year.<br><br>
I will keep tracking this and update you as the picture develops.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors holding Canadian energy ETFs or individual producer stocks:</strong> likely assume the Hormuz story is fully resolved based on headline coverage and have not factored the August 16 expiry into their risk assessment.</p>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors who exited energy positions on the truce news:</strong> may have moved too early if they read the ceasefire as a permanent resolution rather than a sixty-day negotiating window.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I noticed most of the coverage on the Hormuz reopening treats it as a settled story, but the actual agreement only guarantees toll-free shipping for sixty days, which puts the real test of this deal in mid-August. Have you factored that date into how you''re thinking about your energy holdings?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed investor reading headline coverage of the Hormuz truce has no easy way to find the specific terms of the underlying memorandum, including the sixty-day toll-free window and the unresolved question of what happens after it expires. That gap between headline and text is exactly where avoidable portfolio risk accumulates.</p>
<p>An advisor who tracks the actual treaty language, the asymmetric exposure across Gulf producers, and how that connects to Canadian energy equities and the broader inflation outlook offers a level of diligence the DIY investor following news headlines cannot replicate.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Are you currently holding Canadian energy positions, and if so, how did you size them relative to the broader portfolio?</p>
<p>Did you make any changes to your energy holdings based on the Hormuz truce announcement in June?</p>
<p>Are you tracking any specific dates related to the Middle East situation, or has it dropped off your radar since the ceasefire?</p>
<p>Who normally helps you separate headline resolution from the underlying details that could still move markets?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Hormuz truce has an expiry date, and August 16 is worth knowing about<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
Most coverage of the Strait of Hormuz reopening treats the story as resolved. The actual agreement guarantees toll-free shipping for only sixty days, a window that closes around August 16, and Iranian officials have already signaled intent to revisit fees once it expires.<br><br>
If you hold Canadian energy positions, this is a date worth having on your radar regardless of how settled the headlines currently make the situation look.<br><br>
If it would help to walk through what the actual treaty terms say and how they connect to your current positioning, I would be glad to set up a short call.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
