<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The TSX first crossed 35,000 on June 22 on positive US-Iran peace signals and has traded in a tight 34,700 to 35,100 range since, even as oil fell sharply over the same window. Source: TMX Money, BBN Times market coverage.</p>
<h2>Why Banks Are Picking Up the Slack</h2>
<p>Royal Bank, TD, and BMO have all gained over the past several sessions as lower oil prices ease the energy-driven inflation pressure that has kept the Bank of Canada cautious. RBC gained 1% and TD and BMO each rose 0.6% in Monday''s session specifically on the combination of a holding truce and oil prices easing back toward pre-conflict levels. Lower energy costs reduce one of the two competing pressures the Bank of Canada has been weighing all year, inflation risk versus growth weakness, which is a direct tailwind for rate-sensitive financial stocks even without a change in the policy rate itself.</p>
<h2>What This Means Heading Into Wednesday</h2>
<p>Quarter-end positioning adds noise to Monday and Tuesday''s moves, and the TSX''s composition, roughly 70% of total Canadian market capitalization across a relatively small number of large energy and financial names, means a divergence this sharp between the two sectors shows up clearly in the headline index even as it nets out to a modest overall move. The number to watch going forward is not the TSX level itself but whether energy continues to underperform if WTI stabilizes near $70, or whether this week''s Doha talks and the prospect of Iran reasserting leverage after August 16 put a floor back under crude before producers'' second-quarter results land.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients concentrated in Canadian energy are watching a sharp move in oil prices that contradicts the calm, near-record headline level of the TSX, and the disconnect is likely confusing rather than reassuring. Clients in diversified portfolios may not have noticed the divergence at all, since the index-level move has been modest.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to walk you through something happening beneath the surface of the TSX right now. The index itself is calm, sitting close to its record high. But that calm is masking a real divergence: oil prices have collapsed back to where they were before the Iran conflict even started, while Canadian bank stocks are rallying on the same news. If you''re concentrated in energy names, you''re feeling a real hit that the headline index isn''t showing. The fundamentals for Canadian energy producers remain sound even at this oil price, but the free cash flow story that drove this year''s gains gets less powerful below $80 oil. Let''s look at how your specific positioning is affected and whether any rebalancing makes sense heading into the second half.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated positions in Canadian energy producers, particularly those who added exposure during the elevated oil price environment earlier this year.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with diversified Canadian equity exposure who hold both energy and financials, where the two positions are currently moving in opposite directions.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients positioned in Canadian banks, who are benefiting directly from lower energy-driven inflation expectations easing pressure on the Bank of Canada.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client portfolios for concentrated energy sector exposure and quantify the impact of WTI trading near $70 versus the $85 to $95 range assumed in recent positioning decisions.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm whether any clients added energy exposure specifically on the geopolitical risk premium thesis, which has now substantially unwound.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag the August 16 Hormuz toll-free window expiry as a date that could reverse this move and revisit positioning ahead of it.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Monitor this week''s Doha talks for any signal that could re-introduce the risk premium oil has just shed.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The divergence between oil prices and the TSX, and what it means for your energy exposure<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our conversation about the current market divergence. WTI crude has fallen back to pre-conflict levels near $70, its worst quarterly performance since 2020, even though the underlying situation in the Strait of Hormuz remains unresolved. Meanwhile, the TSX itself is holding close to its record high, as bank stocks rally on the same news that is pressuring energy names.<br><br>
If you are concentrated in Canadian energy, this is worth discussing directly, since the index-level calm does not reflect what is happening in that specific sector. I am keeping an eye on the Doha talks this week and the August 16 expiry of the Hormuz toll-free window, either of which could shift this picture again.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
