</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors holding Canadian energy stocks bought during the price spike:</strong> may be watching unrealized gains erode as oil falls back to pre-conflict levels without understanding why, or what it means for their original investment thesis.</p>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors checking only the TSX headline number:</strong> may believe markets are calm and uneventful, missing the significant sector rotation happening underneath that headline figure.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I noticed oil just had its worst quarter since 2020, even though the TSX itself looks calm near its record high. If you hold any Canadian energy positions, there is a real story happening beneath that headline number worth talking through. Do you have a few minutes this week?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed investor checking only the TSX composite level would have no reason to suspect anything unusual is happening in their portfolio right now. The headline index is calm. The reality underneath it, a sharp divergence between collapsing oil prices and rallying bank stocks, is exactly the kind of detail that gets missed without active portfolio monitoring.</p>
<p>An advisor who is tracking sector-level moves, not just the index, can identify when a client''s specific holdings are diverging meaningfully from what the headline market appears to be doing, and can have that conversation before the next earnings cycle forces it.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you know how much of your current portfolio is concentrated in Canadian energy specifically?</p>
<p>Have you checked your energy positions recently, or are you mainly tracking the overall TSX level?</p>
<p>When you added energy exposure, was the thesis tied to the elevated oil price environment from the Iran conflict, or to longer-term fundamentals?</p>
<p>Who normally flags sector-level divergences like this for you before they show up in your account statement?</p>
</div>
